  "content": {
    "id": "p23-q1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p23-introduction-text",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "So far, we have covered functions that are themselves odd or even functions. We also covered functions that were neither. However, it turns out that actually many ‘neither’ functions have a close relationship with odd and even functions. This investigation task aims to show what this relationship is.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p23-definition-stem-text",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "f(x)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " be any function with domain being all real ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "x",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Furthermore, define the following functions.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p23-definition-even-math",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "center",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "E(x)=\\frac{f(x)+f(-x)}{2}",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p23-definition-odd-math",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "center",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "O(x)=\\frac{f(x)-f(-x)}{2}",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $E(x)$ is an even function and that $O(x)$ is an odd function.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$E(-x)=E(x)$, so $E$ is even. $O(-x)=-O(x)$, so $O$ is odd.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}E(-x)&=\\frac{f(-x)+f(-(-x))}{2}\\\\E(-x)&=\\frac{f(-x)+f(x)}{2}\\\\E(-x)&=E(x).\\end{align*}$$ Hence $E$ is even.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}O(-x)&=\\frac{f(-x)-f(-(-x))}{2}\\\\O(-x)&=\\frac{f(-x)-f(x)}{2}\\\\O(-x)&=-\\frac{f(x)-f(-x)}{2}\\\\O(-x)&=-O(x).\\end{align*}$$ Hence $O$ is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 31
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $f(x)=E(x)+O(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$E(x)+O(x)=\\dfrac{2f(x)}{2}=f(x)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}E(x)+O(x)&=\\frac{f(x)+f(-x)}{2}+\\frac{f(x)-f(-x)}{2}\\\\E(x)+O(x)&=\\frac{2f(x)}{2}\\\\E(x)+O(x)&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What is the significance of this result?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Every function defined on all real numbers can be written as the sum of an even function and an odd function.",
          "worked": "Part (a) establishes that $E$ is even and $O$ is odd. Part (b) establishes that their sum is $f$. Thus every function on $\\mathbb R$, including one that is neither even nor odd, has an even component and an odd component.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      }
    ]
  },
